    summary: 'A military unit rarely has to build trust from zero — shared training, a rank structure everyone already respects, and a common mission do most of that work before a new officer ever walks in. Lencioni\'s short fable exists precisely because almost nothing in a civilian workplace offers you that head start, and the team you inherit or build in your first corporate role will need every one of these dysfunctions diagnosed and deliberately dismantled, one at a time, by you, without a system already doing the work in the background.\n\nLencioni tells his argument as a story rather than a manual: a fictional executive team, stocked with genuinely talented, experienced individuals, is nonetheless badly underperforming, and a new leader is brought in to work out why and rebuild it from the ground up. The fable device is doing real work here, not just softening the medicine — it lets you watch the five dysfunctions show up as ordinary, recognisable behaviour before the second half of the book lays out the underlying model directly: people staying carefully quiet in meetings rather than raising an objection, decisions that get nodded through in the room and then quietly ignored the moment everyone is back at their desk, standards that everyone privately knows are not being met but that nobody will actually name out loud.\n\nThe model itself is a pyramid, and the single most important thing to understand about it is that the five dysfunctions are not five independent problems you could tackle in any order — they are causally stacked, each one manufacturing the conditions for the next. At the base sits absence of trust, and Lencioni is precise about what kind of trust he means, because it is not the kind most teams already think they have. It is not confidence that a colleague will hit their deadlines or do competent work; that is predictability, not trust. What he means is vulnerability-based trust: team members being genuinely willing to admit a mistake, a weakness, a gap in their own knowledge, or a need for help, out loud, in front of colleagues, without fear that it will quietly be filed away and used against them later. Almost nothing else in the model is possible without this foundation in place first.\n\nIts absence produces the second dysfunction directly: fear of conflict. A team that has not built vulnerability-based trust cannot risk open, even passionate disagreement about ideas — disagreement feels too dangerous without trust underneath it — so instead the team defaults to guarded, careful comments and what Lencioni calls artificial harmony: meetings that feel pleasant and cooperative on the surface while starving every decision of the honest friction that would actually have improved it. This absence of real debate produces the third dysfunction, lack of commitment: people who never got a genuine chance to voice disagreement rarely commit fully to a decision even when they nodded along to it in the room; they comply quietly and then disengage just as quietly, which is far harder to spot and fix than open resistance would be. Weak, unspoken commitment then produces the fourth dysfunction, avoidance of accountability: without a decision everyone genuinely signed up to, team members become reluctant to call out a colleague whose behaviour or performance is falling short, because doing so means pointing back to a standard that was never really shared in the first place, only nominally agreed to. The fifth and final dysfunction, inattention to results, is the cumulative cost of the previous four: without real peer-to-peer accountability, individual concerns — status, ego, protecting one\'s own department, personal career trajectory — quietly crowd out attention to whatever the team was actually supposed to achieve together.\n\nBecause each dysfunction is built directly on the one beneath it, Lencioni\'s implicit prescription cuts against how most leaders instinctively try to fix a struggling team. A leader who notices "we have an accountability problem" and tries to solve it directly, with new scorecards, sharper performance reviews, or more explicit consequences, is treating a downstream symptom while leaving its actual cause, thin trust and avoided conflict, completely untouched, which is why so many well-intentioned accountability initiatives quietly fail to change anything. The book\'s practical starting point, returned to again and again, is that the leader has to move first: modelling vulnerability themselves, admitting a mistake or an actual gap in their own knowledge openly and without defensiveness, because a team essentially never becomes more vulnerable with each other than its leader has visibly been willing to be. Alongside that, the leader has to deliberately create space for real disagreement rather than reading calm, agreeable meetings as a sign the team is healthy — Lencioni\'s pointed argument is closer to the opposite: a team where every meeting is comfortable is very often a team quietly failing at dysfunction two.\n\nWhat changes in practice if you apply this is where you look first when a team you are leading is not performing. Instead of assuming a results problem needs a results fix, or an accountability gap needs a stricter accountability system, you check the foundation first: whether the team actually has enough trust to argue honestly with each other about real disagreements, not just to be polite in front of each other. For someone arriving from a military background, this is a pointed and slightly uncomfortable translation: a unit typically inherits a working baseline of trust and cohesion from shared training, common risk, and an established rank structure long before a new leader ever takes over it, while a newly assembled civilian team starts with none of that scaffolding in place and has to build every layer of it deliberately, person by person, starting with trust — not assumed, and not skipped past on the way to results.\n\nA leader trying to apply this cannot simply wait for trust and healthy conflict to emerge on their own; Lencioni is explicit that the leader has to actively mine for conflict — deliberately surfacing disagreements that team members would otherwise leave unspoken, naming the elephant in the room rather than letting a meeting end on false consensus, and sometimes playing devil\'s advocate to make sure a comfortable-looking decision has actually been stress-tested before the team commits to it. This runs against a natural leadership instinct to keep meetings calm and efficient, but Lencioni\'s argument is that calm is not the same as healthy, and a team that never disagrees openly is a team quietly building the third dysfunction without anyone noticing.\n\nThe book is also careful about what genuine commitment actually requires, because it is easy to confuse with consensus. Lencioni does not ask a team to agree unanimously before moving forward — waiting for unanimous agreement on every decision is itself a way of avoiding commitment, not achieving it. What he asks for is that everyone has had a real opportunity to be heard and to argue their case before the decision is made, so that even someone who disagrees with the final call can commit to it fully, because they know their view was genuinely considered rather than ignored. Without that sense of having been heard, silent compliance is the best a leader can hope for, and silent compliance is precisely the soil in which the third and fourth dysfunctions take root. Lencioni also points to the value of a simple, visible scoreboard of collective results — something the whole team can see and rally around together — as a small but concrete defence against the fifth dysfunction, because it is much harder for individual ego and status to quietly crowd out the team\'s actual goal when that goal is sitting in front of everyone, tracked and named, rather than left as an abstraction nobody is directly accountable to.',
